  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to transform the minimization problem into a binary optimization task where we seek a binary step function with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum correlation over a shifted version of itself. Unlike previous approaches focusing on continuous optimization, this method leverages binary search techniques. The binary representation constraint is maintained through repair functions, and optimization is achieved through a combination of crossover and repair operations inspired by genetic algorithms, adapted for the discrete nature of the problem. This differs from previous methods in its focus on binary representations and structured neighborhood moves.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe represent h as a binary vector v \u2208 {0,1}^{N} (N=512) with sum(v)=256. The cost is computed as:\n1. corr = np.correlate(v, 1-v, mode=\"full\")\n2. cost = max(corr) * (2.0/512)\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Population initialization (50 vectors):\n   - Include parent solution if available (as-is)\n   - Generate random binary vectors with sum=256 for other 49 members\n   - Ensure all samples within [0,1] and normalized integral\n\n2. Fitness evaluation (for each vector):\n   - Calculate correlation and worst-shift\n   - Maintain best_fitness and best_solution\n\n3. Crossover operation:\n   - Two-point crossover with uniform random breakpoints\n   - Repair to enforce sum=256 if needed\n\n4. Child evaluation:\n   - For each generation, evaluate 2 children\n   - Replace two worst parents with children if they improve the max(worst_shift)\n\n5. Repair subprocesses:\n   - enforce_sum: adjust vector to have exact integral 1\n   - before returning solutions to the optimizer\n\n6. Optimization flow:\n   - Store population vectors (for reuse)\n   - For each generation, compute fitness for untested vectors\n   - Use tournament selection of 3 parents\n   - Evaluate crossover products (2 children)\n   - Accept children if they reduce max(worst_shift)\n\n7. Termination criteria:\n   - Run for ~300 generations (1100s budget)\n   - Return best_fitness found and corresponding vector\n\nSearch Choices and variations:\n- Population size: 50 (good balance between diversity and cost)\n- Repair threshold: strict binary enforcement\n- Crossover probability: always applied\n- Generation limit: ~300\n\nComputational Budget & Robustness:\n- CPU usage: 100% cores during correlation calculations\n- Wall time: ~1100s distributed as:\n  - Correlation (-70%)\n  - Random number generation (~15%)\n  - Enforcement (~15%)\n\n- We'll check remaining time - if below 60s, optimize the best known vector\n- Fallback: return valid vector from parent if time runs out\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Returns vector satisfying all constraints\n- Includes enforce_sum as final repair step\n- Re-evaluate fitness before returning\n- Return format: (vector.tolist(), best_fitness, 512)\n\nHelper functions (numpy required):\n- enforce_sum\n- compute_cost (via correlate)\n- generate_random_binary_vector(n, max_sum)\n- enforce_binary_constraint(v) (clip to 0/1 with repair)",
